{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>Do not become agitated on the way.</b> Rashi explained: do not engage in matters of halakhah. Another interpretation: do not take large strides, and bring the sun into the city. This version is in tractate Ta'anit (10a): Rabbi Elazar said, Joseph said to his brothers: do not engage in matters of halakhah, lest the way become agitated against you. Rashi explained: you will lose your way. Is that so? But did not Rabbi Ila'i say: two scholars who walk on the way and do not speak words of Torah between them are worthy of being burned, etc.? There is no difficulty: this refers to review (l'migras), this refers to analysis (l'iyunei). Rashi explained: for review one needs it on the road, but not for analysis. In a baraita it was taught: do not take large strides, and bring the sun into the city, etc. And our Talmud disagrees with Beresheet Rabbah (95:3), for there it concludes: do not take large strides, and do not refrain from words of Torah, and bring the sun into the city. Now there is room for me to reconcile these two midrashim so that there is no dispute between them. For the girsa — that is, verbal recitation by mouth of straightforward matters whose law is elucidated in the Torah — these are called דִּבְרֵי תּוֹרָה, when one speaks of the fundamental principles of the Torah's laws. But analysis (עִיּוּן) is called דְּבַר הֲלָכָה, for the entire Torah is called a way (דֶּרֶךְ), as it is written (Psalms 119:1): ״אַשְׁרֵי תְמִימֵי דָרֶךְ הַהוֹלְכִים בְּתוֹרַת ה׳״ — and this הֲלִיכָה (walking) is the transfer from place to place, and this is found in the analysis of the wisdom of Torah, for one who investigates it is transferred from level to level, from חָכְמָה to בִּינָה, and from בִּינָה to דַּעַת." "And according to this we can resolve what is concluded in Beresheet Rabbah, ״וְאַל תַּעֲמִידוּ עַצְמְכֶם מִדִּבְרֵי תּוֹרָה״ — that is, to review the laws of the Torah by speaking them while walking on the way. And what is written in the Gemara, ״אַל תִּתְעַסְּקוּ בִּדְבַר הֲלָכָה״ — that is, for analysis, for this is called halakhah, since the investigator goes and is transferred from level to level. And the questioner raises the difficulty from Rabbi Ila'i, who said ״two scholars walking on the way who do not have words of Torah between them,״ etc. — for he had not yet arrived at the distinction between the expression דְּבַר הֲלָכָה and דִּבְרֵי תּוֹרָה, until it was resolved for him: ״this is for review, this is for analysis.״ And the precision of the language supports the resolution as stated. And they do not disagree except in the meaning of the verse ״אַל תִּרְגְּזוּ בַּדָּרֶךְ״ (Genesis 45:24) — whether the intention is that they not ruin their analysis and err in it on account of the fatigue of the way, or the opposite, that they not ruin the simple way itself on account of not engaging in Torah. For the opinion of Rabbi Elazar is that Joseph warned them not to engage in matters of halakhah that require analysis and alertness like the day of the bride's arrival, for there is reason to fear lest the way become agitated against you — on account of the fatigue of the way your heart will be agitated and unsettled, and through this you will come to error in the analysis of divine matters. And this is what Rashi explained, ״תִּתְעוּ״ — that is to say, you will come to error in the analysis of wisdoms. But in Beresheet Rabbah he wanted to explain the opposite — that Joseph came to warn them that they should engage in Torah on the way in straightforward matters, so that they not lose their way on the simple road, for the Torah in which they engage on the way will guide them to their desired destination in peace, as it is written (Proverbs 6:22): ״בְּהִתְהַלֶּכְךָ תַּנְחֶה אֹתָךְ״ etc. And the explanation of the verse ״אַל תִּרְגְּזוּ״ is: do not fear — from the language of ״שָׁמְעוּ עַמִּים יִרְגָּזוּן״ (Exodus 15:14), whose meaning is fear. And he means to say: if you engage in Torah, then you will not need to fear — neither from highwaymen nor from losing your way, for the Torah will guide you and deliver you from all fear and error. As if he said to them: take heed of the matter that saves you from the agitation and fear found on the way — and that is the Torah, which at the time one engages in it protects and delivers." "Another interpretation: the opinion of Beresheet Rabbah is to explain ״אַל תִּרְגְּזוּ״ in the sense of actual agitation and anger, except that a difficulty presented itself to him: what — on the way they should not become agitated, but in their homes they may become agitated? Rather, surely his intent is that on the way you must be especially careful in Torah study — do not refrain from words of Torah even for a single hour — in order to save you from all the calamities that stir and come upon the way. And agitation ruins study, for all anger leads to error, and if you become agitated then you will inevitably have to interrupt your words of Torah lest you come to error through the agitation. Therefore, ״אַל תִּרְגְּזוּ בַּדָּרֶךְ״ — so that you not need to refrain from words of Torah and interrupt during the time of anger. And because in a place of danger a person needs extra exhortation, therefore it states ״בַּדָּרֶךְ״; but in the house there is not such strictness, even though in truth agitation is harmful everywhere." "And there are those who explain ״אַל תִּרְגְּזוּ בִּדְבַר הֲלָכָה״ in the language of our Rabbis of blessed memory (Zevachim 47a): ״מִתְעַסֵּק בַּקֳּדָשִׁים פָּסוּל״ (one who acts unthinkingly with consecrated offerings is disqualified) — and it speaks of one who does not study with analysis but merely engages casually without intention. And perhaps their intent is to say that agitation will bring them to this and they will be close to error. Now, in the baraita the Tanna taught ״אַל תִּפְסְעוּ פְּסִיעָה גַסָּה וְהַכְנִיסוּ חַמָּה לָעִיר״ — their opinion is to explain the agitation as meaning haste and rushing, from the language of ״פַּחַז כַּמַּיִם״ (Genesis 49:4), which speaks of the haste of agitation. Because Joseph said to them ״מַהֲרוּ וַעֲלוּ אֶל אָבִי״ (Genesis 45:9) — that is to say, hurry to go from here and do not remain here — he was concerned lest they understand from the expression ״מַהֲרוּ״ that also on the way they should not walk at a measured pace but rather in haste, in order to hasten the tidings to their father. And through this they would take strides larger than customary, and would walk until evening, even after sunset. Therefore he said to them, ״אַל תִּרְגְּזוּ בַּדָּרֶךְ״ — from here, yes, ״מַהֲרוּ וַעֲלוּ״; but on the way itself, do not rush, but walk at a measured pace with moderate strides, and enter the city while the sun is still shining, for one should always enter while it is still good." "And there are those who say, since it is stated (Ecclesiastes 2:9): ״אַף חָכְמָתִי עָמְדָה לִּי״ — that which I learned in anger and wrath — and regarding this he said ״אַל תִּרְגְּזוּ בַּדָּרֶךְ״ — to learn wisdom in anger. And perhaps they intended a matter that requires much analysis as stated above. And I myself add a good lesson of good taste and understanding: since all anger leads to error, and our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Gittin 43a): ״אֵין אָדָם עוֹמֵד עַל דְּבַר הֲלָכָה אֶלָּא אִם כֵּן נִכְשַׁל בָּהּ תְּחִלָּה״ (a person does not stand firm on a matter of halakhah unless he has first stumbled in it). Therefore they said that the wisdom I learned in anger and wrath, which brought me to error — that is what stood by me, for one who errs in a matter once is afterward all the more careful against it for the future."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 45, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:34:01.353662923Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}